<h2>Common ISFDB Misconceptions</h2>

There are a number of misconceptions held by some members of the public
about the ISFDB, many of which are conveniently summarized in this email 
received from the author
<a href="http://DBASELOC/exact_author.cgi?Michael_Tobias">Michael Tobias</a>:
<p>
<ul>
<i>
PLEASE NOTE:
<br>
Your prominent bibliography summary for Michael Tobias is vastly incomplete 
and leads to a false impression. An extensive correction was sent two weeks 
ago and remains unintegrated. Michael Tobias is the author of nearly 30 books 
and over 100 films -all published and/or broadcast. Yet, your site lists only 
two books. Please correct ASAP or remove Mr. Tobias' name from your 
website.Thank you,
<p>
Michael Tobias
</i>
</ul>
<p>

Here is a presentation of the facts, as opposed to the misconceptions contained
within the above email:

<ol>
<li><i>The ISFDB is not comprehensive.</i> Our primary goal here is to catalog
publications. This is done in such a way that it makes it easy to display a summary
bibliograpy for each author, but the fact remains that data is centered around
publications, not authors. Thus, if we are missing a particular publication, then
the author bibliographies which are touched by that publication will be incomplete.
We know for a fact that we a missing thousands of publications in the ISFDB. Hence,
many - if not all - of the summary bibliographies are incomplete. Some vastly so.
QED.
<p>

<li><i>The ISFDB is a database of Speculative Fiction.</i> As stated elsewhere, we
don't catalog films, and we're not especially interested in cataloging printed matter
of a non-SFnal nature. If the author is a major figure in the SF field (for instance,
they've been awarded a Grand Master or Lifetime Achievement award), then we could be
swayed into cataloging non-SFnal matter in an effort to complete the bibliography of
a major SF figure. But otherwise, we're not interested in cataloging an article about
the Brady Bunch that an author might have written for <i>TV Guide</i>.
<p>

<li><i>The ISFDB is not updated according to a schedule.</i> The ISFDB has no
paid staff, and those who work on the database do so because they find it an
interesting hobby. As with any hobby, life can and will impose itself on one's
free time, greatly slowing the integration of any submissions made to the ISFDB.
As we all know from recent events, the length of time it takes to integrate data
into the ISFDB can sometimes be measured in years - not days or weeks.
<p>

<li><i>The ISFDB has its own list of priorities.</i> Some data has a much higher
priority for integration into the ISFDB than other data, and we aren't much inclined
to drop everything in order to bring a specific author's bibliography up to date.
In order of priority, the data we intend to integrate are:
<ol>
<p>
<li> <b>Forthcoming books</b>. Aside from serving as a convenient collation of
various far-flung forthcoming book information, this data also serves as the
first entry of new titles into the database. By tracking forthcoming books
from the various SF imprints, we are populating the database with most of
the titles that will be of concern in the future. Since this data concerns
future events, it is the most perishable of all data in the ISFDB, and hence
has the highest priority. The ISFDB has staff dedicated exclusively to the
tracking of forthcoming books.
<p>
<li> <b>Awards</b>. The recording of award information forces us to track down
high-quality works not present in the database, especially short fiction which
has no representation in forthcoming books. This data is also used
to construct the various reading lists support by the ISFDB. While award data
can be found elsewhere online, it is not integrated into general bibliographic
information as it is here at the ISFDB. The ISFDB has staff dedicated exclusively
to the tracking of awards information.
<p>
<li> <b>Current book reviews from SFnal periodicals</b>. Since print magazines
are short on space, they tend to review either books of high quality
or of high interest. In any case, we use this information to fill in
gaps in our bibliographic coverage missed in our forthcoming books data.
<p>
<li> <b>Novels published prior to 1984.</b> The
<a href="http://www.locusmag.com/index">Locus Database</a> has excellent coverage
of the SF genre since 1984. Indices of novels prior to 1984, however, do not
exist online, and novel indices in print have gaps either in coverage or time.
<p>
<li> <b>Magazines published prior to 1984.</b> Again, the
<a href="http://www.locusmag.com/index">Locus Database</a> has excellent coverage
of the SF genre, including magazines, since 1984. Indices of magazines prior to 1984, 
however, do not exist online. Several indices exist in print, hence the reduced
priority here.
<p>
<li> <b>Interior artwork.</b> There are no indices of interior artwork either
online nor in print.
</ol>
<p>
Data of low priority for integration into the ISFDB:
<ol>
<p>
<li> <b>Anthologies and Collections.</b> Anthologies and collection published
since 1984 are well covered by the
<a href="http://www.locusmag.com/index">Locus Database</a>. Anthologies and
collections published prior to 1984 are well covered by Contento's
<a href="http://www.best.com/~contento/">Index to Science
Fiction Anthologies and Collections</a>. 
<p>
<li> <b>Non-current works published after 1984.</b> As noted above, this data is
covered elsewhere online.
<p>
<li> <b>Individual author bibliographies.</b> It simply doesn't make sense to
do this kind of polish work until we've pulled in most of the books and magazines
of the genre.
</ol>
<p>

<li><i>Unsolicited submissions to ISFDB are not guaranteed to be integrated.</i>
While best efforts are made to integrate data which is submitted online (currently
limited to author biographic data only), data which is dropped off in the email
box will be addressed when time permits. Such data, which is usually free-form text,
has to be transmuted into the various ISFDB data formats by hand, which is a 
time-consuming process.
<p>

<li><i>The ISFDB will make the editorial decision on whether material is appropriate 
for integration into the ISFDB.</i> In general, material which is a matter of public
record will not be removed from the ISFDB. If an author wishes an email address or
web site to be removed from their biographic area, we will conform to their wishes.
If someone has posted copyrighted material (perhaps inappropriately lifting a synopsis
from a copyrighted source) and it is brought to our attention, we will remove the
material. Submissions of an offensive nature - such as deriding an author's works
or lifestyle, or synopsis' which contain offensive or vulgar language - will not be
integrated. We will not, however, alter or remove published titles that contain offensive
or vulgar language.
<p>

<li><i>The ISFDB is not an author service.</i> If it were, we would be billing for
services rendered. While we do not mind linking external pages directly into an
author's bibliography, that ability should not be misconstrued as an obligation on
our part to keep that bibliography up to date. See points #1 and #4 above.
<p>

</ol>


